%description:

TcpCubic.hystartDetect:invalid -- a bitmask above 3 stops the run with an error.

The claim: the code accepts 0 to 3.

The classes: none (0), train (1), delay (2), both (3, the default, the value of Linux
tcp_cubic.c), invalid (below 0, above 3). The code: HyStart acts in slow start when the window is at least hystartLowWindow segments
(TcpCubic::processRttSample()). TcpCubic::hystartUpdate() looks for the exit: detector 1, the
ACK train, and detector 2, the delay increase, whose threshold is the minimum RTT / 8, clamped
to hystartDelayMin and hystartDelayMax (TcpCubic::hystartDelayThresh()). At the exit, ssthresh
becomes the window. TcpCubic::initializeAlgorithm() checks the values.

Host under test: host1, with TcpCubic. Observation: the error, and the exit code of the run.

%# parameter: TcpCubic.hystartDetect
%# class: invalid
%# value: 4
%# key: *.host1.tcp.conn-*.algorithm.hystartDetect
%# expected-result: PASS

%activity:
EV << "TcpCubicHystartDetectInvalid compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.conn-*.algorithm.hystartDetect = 4    # the parameter under test
*.host1.tcp.tcpAlgorithmClass = "TcpCubic"
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%exitcode: 1

%contains-regex: stderr
hystartDetect must be 0 to 3, but it is 4
